1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,800
These two beakers both read pH four point

2
00:00:02,960 --> 00:00:04,080
six eight.

3
00:00:04,160 --> 00:00:06,320
One is a buffer you've just made.

4
00:00:06,400 --> 00:00:09,600
The other is very dilute hydrochloric acid.

5
00:00:09,680 --> 00:00:10,960
Which is which?

6
00:00:11,040 --> 00:00:12,960
You can't tell from the reading, because a

7
00:00:13,040 --> 00:00:15,680
buffer isn't defined by its pH.

8
00:00:15,720 --> 00:00:18,080
It's defined by what it does.

9
00:00:18,240 --> 00:00:20,773
So in this practical you'll make a buffer,

10
00:00:20,827 --> 00:00:21,840
and then prove it.

11
00:00:23,067 --> 00:00:25,666
From the buffers video, a buffer needs a

12
00:00:25,707 --> 00:00:28,506
weak acid and its conjugate base, both in

13
00:00:28,587 --> 00:00:29,546
large amounts.

14
00:00:30,027 --> 00:00:33,146
We'll use ethanoic acid, and sodium ethanoate to

15
00:00:33,227 --> 00:00:34,426
supply the ethanoate.

16
00:00:35,067 --> 00:00:37,386
Mix equal moles of each, and the ratio

17
00:00:37,467 --> 00:00:39,946
is one, so the pH should come out

18
00:00:39,987 --> 00:00:42,986
at the pKa: about four point seven four.

19
00:00:44,200 --> 00:00:47,480
Use a volumetric pipette to transfer fifty millilitres

20
00:00:47,600 --> 00:00:50,520
of each solution into a clean, dry beaker,

21
00:00:50,600 --> 00:00:51,800
and swirl.

22
00:00:51,880 --> 00:00:55,000
That's one hundred millilitres, with zero point zero

23
00:00:55,160 --> 00:00:58,760
zero five zero zero moles of each partner.

24
00:00:58,800 --> 00:01:02,600
Pipettes matter, because the ratio sets the pH.

25
00:01:02,680 --> 00:01:05,320
And make enough: each set of tests uses

26
00:01:05,520 --> 00:01:08,680
about seventy millilitres, so fifty would leave nothing

27
00:01:08,760 --> 00:01:10,520
for rinsing or a re-test.

28
00:01:11,734 --> 00:01:14,213
Measure it with the calibrated meter, using the

29
00:01:14,294 --> 00:01:16,053
routine from the pH practical.

30
00:01:16,694 --> 00:01:19,493
One class's mean was four point six eight,

31
00:01:19,534 --> 00:01:21,373
a little below the prediction.

32
00:01:21,654 --> 00:01:24,373
The simple ratio calculation treats the ions as

33
00:01:24,454 --> 00:01:26,613
if they don't affect each other, and real

34
00:01:26,694 --> 00:01:28,613
ions do, a little.

35
00:01:28,934 --> 00:01:31,253
What matters here is how much the pH

36
00:01:31,334 --> 00:01:32,053
changes.

37
00:01:33,267 --> 00:01:35,426
Use a pipette to split the buffer into

38
00:01:35,587 --> 00:01:39,346
twenty millilitre portions, with matching twenty millilitre portions

39
00:01:39,427 --> 00:01:40,866
of distilled water.

40
00:01:41,187 --> 00:01:44,386
Water is the control: same treatment, but not

41
00:01:44,467 --> 00:01:45,426
buffered.

42
00:01:45,587 --> 00:01:48,306
Read each starting pH, then add the same

43
00:01:48,387 --> 00:01:51,426
dose to each pair: one millilitre of zero

44
00:01:51,587 --> 00:01:54,386
point one zero zero moles per litre hydrochloric

45
00:01:54,547 --> 00:01:57,186
acid, or sodium hydroxide.

46
00:01:57,307 --> 00:02:00,066
Acid and base go into different portions, because

47
00:02:00,147 --> 00:02:02,946
base added after acid would just undo it.

48
00:02:04,167 --> 00:02:06,246
The buffer went from four point six eight

49
00:02:06,487 --> 00:02:09,046
to four point five nine with acid, and

50
00:02:09,127 --> 00:02:11,366
to four point seven seven with base.

51
00:02:11,847 --> 00:02:13,606
That's less than a tenth of a unit

52
00:02:13,687 --> 00:02:14,326
either way.

53
00:02:14,967 --> 00:02:17,606
The water started at five point nine three,

54
00:02:17,727 --> 00:02:20,326
because of dissolved carbon dioxide, so it had

55
00:02:20,407 --> 00:02:22,166
further to rise than to fall.

56
00:02:22,727 --> 00:02:25,046
It plunged to two point three five with

57
00:02:25,127 --> 00:02:27,686
acid, and shot up to eleven point six

58
00:02:27,847 --> 00:02:28,886
three with base.

59
00:02:29,367 --> 00:02:31,206
The buffer resists the change.

60
00:02:31,447 --> 00:02:32,646
It doesn't prevent it.

61
00:02:33,867 --> 00:02:35,106
Why so little?

62
00:02:35,387 --> 00:02:38,426
Each buffer portion holds zero point zero zero

63
00:02:38,667 --> 00:02:41,706
one zero zero moles of each partner, and

64
00:02:41,787 --> 00:02:43,706
the acid dose is only one tenth of

65
00:02:43,787 --> 00:02:44,266
that.

66
00:02:44,507 --> 00:02:46,746
It's the buffers video's example at a tenth

67
00:02:46,827 --> 00:02:50,346
of the scale: ethanoate takes up the hydronium,

68
00:02:50,387 --> 00:02:52,506
the ratio moves from one to about one

69
00:02:52,587 --> 00:02:55,386
point two, and the predicted pH falls to

70
00:02:55,467 --> 00:02:57,146
four point six six.

71
00:02:57,227 --> 00:02:59,226
That's the same small drop you measured.

72
00:03:00,434 --> 00:03:03,633
Now a property an unbuffered acid can't copy.

73
00:03:03,954 --> 00:03:06,353
Dilute ten millilitres of buffer to a hundred

74
00:03:06,434 --> 00:03:09,313
millilitres in a volumetric flask, just like the

75
00:03:09,474 --> 00:03:10,193
strength practical.

76
00:03:10,754 --> 00:03:13,553
Both partners are diluted equally, so the ratio

77
00:03:13,634 --> 00:03:16,193
stays at one, and the pH should barely

78
00:03:16,394 --> 00:03:16,673
move.

79
00:03:17,314 --> 00:03:19,393
It went from four point six eight to

80
00:03:19,474 --> 00:03:21,153
four point seven two.

81
00:03:21,194 --> 00:03:24,833
Diluting hydrochloric acid tenfold moved its pH about

82
00:03:24,914 --> 00:03:25,953
a whole unit.

83
00:03:27,167 --> 00:03:30,046
But the diluted buffer has paid a price.

84
00:03:30,127 --> 00:03:32,446
A twenty millilitre portion of it now holds

85
00:03:32,527 --> 00:03:34,846
only a tenth of the partners, so the

86
00:03:35,007 --> 00:03:37,486
same one millilitre of acid uses up all

87
00:03:37,567 --> 00:03:38,766
its ethanoate.

88
00:03:38,847 --> 00:03:40,046
And the pH?

89
00:03:40,127 --> 00:03:42,446
It fell from four point seven two to

90
00:03:42,607 --> 00:03:44,846
three point four one, more than a whole

91
00:03:45,007 --> 00:03:45,966
unit.

92
00:03:46,087 --> 00:03:49,006
Same pH as before, much less capacity.

93
00:03:50,234 --> 00:03:51,673
So, the variables.

94
00:03:51,754 --> 00:03:55,753
The independent variable is what's being tested, buffer

95
00:03:55,834 --> 00:03:59,033
or water, with acid, base, or dilution.

96
00:03:59,594 --> 00:04:02,473
The dependent variable is the change in pH.

97
00:04:02,714 --> 00:04:05,113
And you control the portion volume, the dose

98
00:04:05,194 --> 00:04:08,153
of acid or base, the temperature, the calibrated

99
00:04:08,234 --> 00:04:10,233
meter and the rinsing routine.

100
00:04:11,434 --> 00:04:14,353
Validity: does the test show buffering?

101
00:04:14,634 --> 00:04:16,793
Only because the water control gets the same

102
00:04:16,874 --> 00:04:19,513
dose in the same volume, and every test

103
00:04:19,594 --> 00:04:21,353
uses a fresh portion.

104
00:04:21,394 --> 00:04:24,473
An even closer control is that dilute hydrochloric

105
00:04:24,554 --> 00:04:27,913
acid from the start: same pH, same dose,

106
00:04:27,954 --> 00:04:29,673
and it would plunge to about two point

107
00:04:29,834 --> 00:04:30,793
three.

108
00:04:30,914 --> 00:04:34,793
Reliability: three fresh batches agreed within zero point

109
00:04:34,874 --> 00:04:36,153
zero two.

110
00:04:36,474 --> 00:04:39,513
Accuracy: calibrate the meter, and measure volumes with

111
00:04:39,594 --> 00:04:40,553
pipettes.

112
00:04:40,594 --> 00:04:43,513
And because you compare changes, a constant offset

113
00:04:43,634 --> 00:04:45,513
in every reading cancels out.

114
00:04:46,734 --> 00:04:48,133
The risk assessment.

115
00:04:48,414 --> 00:04:51,293
The dilute acids and sodium ethanoate are well

116
00:04:51,454 --> 00:04:54,733
below their classification limits, but wear safety glasses

117
00:04:54,814 --> 00:04:55,533
anyway.

118
00:04:55,854 --> 00:04:58,493
Zero point one zero zero moles per litre

119
00:04:58,534 --> 00:05:02,333
sodium hydroxide sits just under the irritant limit,

120
00:05:02,414 --> 00:05:04,573
so treat a splash as harmful.

121
00:05:04,814 --> 00:05:08,653
Glacial ethanoic acid, the concentrated stock, is flammable

122
00:05:08,814 --> 00:05:11,373
and corrosive, and stays with your teacher.

123
00:05:11,774 --> 00:05:13,853
And always use a pipette filler.

124
00:05:15,067 --> 00:05:19,066
Here's the method to remember: split, dose, compare.

125
00:05:19,707 --> 00:05:22,426
Split into fresh portions, give the buffer and

126
00:05:22,467 --> 00:05:24,666
the water the same dose, and compare the

127
00:05:24,747 --> 00:05:25,466
changes.

128
00:05:26,667 --> 00:05:27,306
Your turn.

129
00:05:27,867 --> 00:05:30,506
A student adds one millilitre of acid to

130
00:05:30,667 --> 00:05:33,386
twenty millilitres of a solution, and the pH

131
00:05:33,467 --> 00:05:35,546
goes from four point six eight to four

132
00:05:35,707 --> 00:05:37,306
point five nine.

133
00:05:37,387 --> 00:05:39,386
Then she adds the same amount of sodium

134
00:05:39,467 --> 00:05:42,346
hydroxide to the same beaker, and it returns

135
00:05:42,427 --> 00:05:44,186
to four point six eight.

136
00:05:44,267 --> 00:05:46,506
She concludes it's a buffer.

137
00:05:46,827 --> 00:05:48,186
Evaluate her method.

138
00:05:48,347 --> 00:05:49,546
Pause here to answer.

139
00:05:59,967 --> 00:06:02,126
The small drop fits a buffer, but the

140
00:06:02,207 --> 00:06:04,006
method has two flaws.

141
00:06:04,207 --> 00:06:07,006
There's no control: she needs the same additions

142
00:06:07,087 --> 00:06:09,246
to twenty millilitres of water to show the

143
00:06:09,407 --> 00:06:11,326
change is unusually small.

144
00:06:11,487 --> 00:06:13,966
And the base went into the same beaker,

145
00:06:14,007 --> 00:06:16,526
so it just neutralised the acid she'd added.

146
00:06:16,567 --> 00:06:18,526
That return to four point six eight tells

147
00:06:18,607 --> 00:06:20,366
you nothing about base.

148
00:06:20,447 --> 00:06:21,806
Use a fresh portion.

149
00:06:23,034 --> 00:06:23,873
One more.

150
00:06:24,154 --> 00:06:27,273
A student mixes thirty millilitres of zero point

151
00:06:27,354 --> 00:06:30,393
one zero zero moles per litre ethanoic acid

152
00:06:30,794 --> 00:06:33,673
with ten millilitres of zero point one zero

153
00:06:33,794 --> 00:06:36,713
zero moles per litre sodium ethanoate at twenty

154
00:06:36,778 --> 00:06:38,313
five degrees.

155
00:06:38,594 --> 00:06:40,553
Calculate the expected pH.

156
00:06:40,874 --> 00:06:43,033
K a is one point eight times ten

157
00:06:43,114 --> 00:06:44,713
to the minus five.

158
00:06:44,874 --> 00:06:46,073
Pause here to answer.

159
00:06:56,500 --> 00:06:58,020
Find the moles first.

160
00:06:58,260 --> 00:07:02,020
Ethanoic acid, zero point zero zero three zero

161
00:07:02,140 --> 00:07:03,220
zero moles.

162
00:07:03,460 --> 00:07:07,380
Ethanoate, zero point zero zero one zero zero.

163
00:07:07,420 --> 00:07:09,260
So the ratio is three.

164
00:07:09,540 --> 00:07:12,900
Hydronium is K a times three, five point

165
00:07:13,020 --> 00:07:15,220
four times ten to the minus five, and

166
00:07:15,300 --> 00:07:17,780
the pH is four point two seven.

167
00:07:17,860 --> 00:07:19,780
It's not four point seven four.

168
00:07:19,860 --> 00:07:21,460
That's only for equal amounts.

169
00:07:22,667 --> 00:07:25,706
So, to prepare and test a buffer: mix

170
00:07:25,787 --> 00:07:27,946
a weak acid and its conjugate base in

171
00:07:28,027 --> 00:07:31,546
measured amounts, predict its pH from the ratio,

172
00:07:31,667 --> 00:07:34,506
then split it, dose it alongside water, and

173
00:07:34,587 --> 00:07:35,706
compare the changes.

174
00:07:36,187 --> 00:07:38,426
Check dilution, and capacity too.

175
00:07:39,307 --> 00:07:42,586
Next: titration, where a measured volume of one

176
00:07:42,667 --> 00:07:45,466
solution reveals the concentration of another.

177
00:07:46,667 --> 00:07:48,106
Here are the key notes.

178
